   "source": [
    "---\n",
    "## 5. Stage D — selectivity and text alignment\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the **main deliverable**: are SAE features more *selectively* associated with anatomy /\n",
    "pathology / imaging context than the dense embedding dimensions?\n",
    "\n",
    "### 5.1 Selectivity against ground-truth labels, with controls\n",
    "\n",
    "**Choosing a fair metric.** The obvious choice, AUROC, is *structurally biased against sparse\n",
    "features*. A feature that fires on a fraction $f$ of images is zero everywhere else, so all\n",
    "non-firing images tie. Even if every image it fires on is positive, its AUROC is capped at\n",
    "\n",
    "$$\\mathrm{AUROC}_{\\max}=0.5+0.5\\min(1,\\,f/p)$$\n",
    "\n",
    "for a label of prevalence $p$ — a perfectly clean feature firing on 5 % of images against a 20 %\n",
    "label cannot exceed 0.625. Ranking dense dimensions against sparse features by AUROC would\n",
    "therefore \"prove\" that dense dimensions are more selective no matter what the SAE learned.\n",
    "\n",
    "So selectivity is measured with two metrics at **matched coverage**. Every unit — SAE feature, dense\n",
    "dimension or control — is allowed to claim the same number of images, $k$ = the median number of\n",
    "images an SAE feature fires on. Dense dimensions are scored in both signs, taking the better one,\n",
    "so they are not penalised for being negatively predictive.\n",
    "\n",
    "1. **Purity** — precision@k: of a unit's top-$k$ images, what fraction carry the attribute? Read it\n",
    "   against the attribute's base rate. This is what you are implicitly judging when you look at a\n",
    "   top-activating image grid, and it is the metric the outline names.\n",
    "2. **Monosemanticity** — how many *different* attributes is one unit enriched for (lift ≥ 2)?\n",
    "   Purity alone saturates on an easy corpus: many units in every bank reach precision 1.0, so\n",
    "   \"which bank is more selective\" is not decided by purity. The number of attributes a single unit\n",
    "   responds to is the discriminating question, and it is the one the phrase *\"more selectively\n",
    "   associated\"* in the research question actually asks. Fewer is better.\n",
    "\n",
    "AUROC is reported as a secondary number, with its sparsity ceiling shown alongside.\n",
    "\n",
    "Three controls make the comparison honest — the outline's warning about over-claiming is easy to\n",
    "trip over here, because the SAE has 8× more units than the dense space and *more units means a\n",
    "higher maximum by chance alone*:\n",
    "\n",
    "| Control | Purpose |\n",
    "|---|---|\n",
    "| **Random ReLU features** | same width & same L0 as the SAE, untrained → is the gain from *learning* or from *width + sparsity*? |\n",
    "| **Shuffled labels** | destroys the label relationship → what maximum AUROC does a bank of this size reach by chance? |\n",
    "| **Nuisance attributes** | view / protocol / scanner as targets → how many \"clean\" features encode acquisition, not anatomy? |"
   ]
  },
