      "\n",
      "VERDICT on the main research question\n",
      "\n",
      "  (1) purity - pathology attributes, best SAE feature vs best dense dim (precision@52):\n",
      "      SAE better 2 | tied (within 0.02) 0 | dense better 4   of 6\n",
      "      mean best precision:  SAE 0.837 | dense 0.891 | random 0.821 | chance 0.236\n",
      "      -> dense ahead\n",
      "\n",
      "  (2) monosemanticity - attributes per informative unit (lower = more selective):\n",
      "      SAE 2.00 | dense 4.72 | random 2.12\n",
      "      units enriched for exactly one attribute:  SAE 32.0% | dense 0.6% | random 21.3%\n",
      "      -> SAE ahead\n",
      "\n",
      "  ANSWER: purity dense ahead, monosemanticity SAE ahead.\n",
      "  Split verdict, and it is the informative one. Dense dimensions are the better\n",
      "  *detectors* - one dimension separates a finding more cleanly. SAE features are the\n",
      "  more *selective* units - each responds to far fewer distinct attributes. The research\n",
      "  question asks about selective association, so this supports the SAE on the axis it\n",
      "  asked about while contradicting any claim that the SAE detects pathology better.\n",
      "  Both halves belong in the write-up; reporting only one would misrepresent the run.\n",
      "\n",
      "  Caveat: the SAE bank has more units than the dense bank, so it gets more chances at a\n",
      "  high score. The shuffled-label null and the width-matched random control price that in.\n"
     ]
