   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "\n",
      "LIMITATIONS - state these wherever the results are shown\n",
      "--------------------------------------------------------\n",
      "1. Data. This run used: synthetic control corpus. A synthetic corpus has known generative factors, which makes\n",
      "   the interpretability claims *checkable*, but it says nothing about real knee MRI. Nothing here\n",
      "   generalises to the RSNA challenge unless it is rerun on that data.\n",
      "2. Encoder. BioMedCLIP is a general biomedical encoder, not a knee-MRI foundation model.\n",
      "3. Geometry. One pooled embedding per 2-D slice discards the 3-D, multi-slice, multi-sequence\n",
      "   structure that real knee reading depends on.\n",
      "4. Confounding. 576 of 1007 usable features (57%) were at\n",
      "   least as enriched for an acquisition attribute (view, pulse sequence, scanner) as for any\n",
      "   pathology. A feature that tracks a label may be encoding acquisition or artifact instead.\n",
      "5. More units means a higher maximum by chance. The shuffled-label null\n",
      "   (best precision@k 0.39 for the SAE bank) and the width- and\n",
      "   L0-matched random-feature control are the honest comparison, not the raw best score.\n",
      "   Note also that AUROC is capped for sparse units (section 5.1), which is why precision@k is primary.\n",
      "6. Faithfulness. QA accuracy from a sparse memory does not show the answer was produced *because of*\n",
      "   the retained features. That needs counterfactual ablation and expert review.\n",
      "7. Naming. Every feature label in this notebook is a CANDIDATE concept. Automated labels are\n",
      "   hypotheses until a qualified reader confirms them.\n",
      "\n",
      "This is an interpretability and representation-learning study. It is not a diagnostic tool and not\n",
      "a clinical decision-support system.\n",
      "\n"
     ]
    },
